scala_mcp_tool — wrap any classpath method as an mcp tool (tier 2)
====================================================================

status: planned

spec: "scala-backed mcp tools: scala_mcp_fun and scala_mcp_tool"
(D-2026-08-19-scala-backed-mcp-tools). decided 2026-08-19, NOT scheduled
into a wave. investigation notes: LINTER_FINDINGS.md.

TIER 2 of two. this plan removes tier 1's precondition. plans/scala_mcp_fun
can only wrap a function its author already registered as a Scala.Fun;
this one wraps ANY class + method on the classpath, so it reaches
components whose author never heard of us.

    scala_mcp_tool lint = \<open>isabelle.linter.Linter.lint_snapshot\<close>
        (description \<open>lint a theory\<close>)
        (params theory: string)

DEPENDS ON plans/scala_mcp_fun and does not restate its steps. that plan
settles the error mapping, the registry/activation story, and the fact
that ML can call scala from inside a tool body at all. what is new here
is one Scala.Fun of ours and the reflection behind it.

it does NOT inherit tier 1's calling convention. tier 1's D1 makes
declaration ORDER the contract and accepts that nothing checks it —
defensible there because its audience wrote both the Fun and the
declaration. tier 2's whole point is code whose author never heard of
us, so that trust is unavailable. what tier 2 inherits is the PROBLEM
statement: mcp arguments arrive named, scala wants them positional, and
something must bridge that. reflection is how tier 2 pays for it —
see D1 below and its open question Q4.

WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL. the isabelle linter is the motivating case and it
is exactly the kind tier 1 cannot reach: it ships
`isabelle.linter.Linter_Tools` (a CLI service) and `isabelle.linter.Lints`
(its own lint extension point), but no Scala.Functions service. Its
engine is one pure function over the object our server already holds:

    (* isabelle-linter linter_base/src/linter.scala:131 *)
    def lint_snapshot(snapshot: Document.Snapshot,
                      lint_selection: Lint_Store.Selection): Report

we must not link against it and it must not know about us. reflection is
what makes both true at once.

NON-GOALS. no enumeration of what is on the classpath — the user names
the target. no attempt to serve the CLI plane (`isabelle lint` and
friends): tool bodies print to stdout and stdout is our transport, and
external tools call sys.exit in their own closure, so that plane stays
discovery-only (see LINTER_FINDINGS.md section 3). no linter-specific
code anywhere in mcp.jar — if this plan ends up mentioning the linter in
source rather than in tests, it has failed.

the one new scala function
--------------------------

    MCP.dynamic_call   (a Scala.Fun in mcp.jar, ours, the only one)

    1. split the target into class + method
    2. Class.forName(cls, true, Isabelle_System.classpath().class_loader)
    3. .getField("MODULE$").get(null)      -- scala object singleton
    4. reflect the signature; fill each parameter (D1); invoke
    5. render the result (D2)

it must extend Scala.Fun DIRECTLY, not Fun_String. this is the load-
bearing detail of the whole plan:

    (* Pure/System/scala.scala:22 *)
    abstract class Fun(val name: String, val thread: Boolean = false) {
      def invoke(session: Session, args: List[Bytes]): List[Bytes]
    }

Fun_String and Fun_Strings are conveniences that DROP the session. we
need it: ML cannot express a Document.Snapshot, but the session can
resolve one by theory name, and without that lint_snapshot is
unreachable no matter how good the reflection is.

the work items
--------------

1. target parsing + declaration-time validation. `scala_mcp_tool` outer
   syntax in MCP_Tools.thy. the target is `pkg.Class.method`; the split
   is ambiguous (dots appear in both halves), so resolve it by trying
   longest-class-first and failing with the candidates listed. validate
   through a new MCP.check_target Fun (class loadable, method present,
   signature fillable per D1) so a typo fails at REGISTRATION with a
   position — the same contract mcp_tool has via
   Outer_Syntax.check_command and scala_mcp_fun has via
   Resources.check_scala_function. verified by A1, A2.

2. MCP.dynamic_call: steps 1-3 above, plus invocation. no coercion yet —
   String-only parameters. this is the smallest thing that runs.
   verified by T1.

3. the fill rule (D1): each parameter is filled either from the ML call
   args or from server state. this is the step that makes the linter
   reachable. verified by T2.

4. coercion table (D2): String => T for the types a real target needs.
   verified by T3.

5. result rendering (D3). verified by T4.

6. tests below; rebuild per rules.

7. close-out: spec checkbox, plans/README status, CHANGELOG entry.

decisions and assumptions
-------------------------

(testing process: bridge cases in mcp_test/src/mcp_bridge_tests.scala
extending MCP_Session_Suite; the scala-unit layer over Fake_Backend can
carry the pure reflection cases, which need no prover. see plans/README.
IMPORTANT: the linter is NOT installed in this environment and must not
become a test dependency — target our OWN jar's classes for every
mechanical case, and keep the linter to one optional, skipped-if-absent
integration case.)

D1  the fill rule, and the reason a signature CHECK is worth having at
    all. UNLIKE tier 1, this does not rest on declaration order — the
    method's own signature is readable, so use it. each parameter is
    filled from one of two sources, decided by its type:
      EXPLICIT  String, Int, Boolean, and anything in the coercion
                table (D2) <- the ML call args, by parameter NAME
      AMBIENT   Document.Snapshot, Session, Options, Sessions.Deps
                <- server state, never from ML
    an ambient parameter that needs a selector (which snapshot?) takes
    it from an explicit param by convention: a Document.Snapshot is
    resolved from a `theory` argument. a parameter that is neither
    explicit nor ambient is a registration error naming the type.
    this is what turns "call string functions" into "call
    lint_snapshot", and it is why the check is dispatch and not merely
    validation.

D2  coercion is the real work, not the plumbing. ML hands over strings;
    a real target wants List[Path], Options, Severity.Level,
    Lint_Store.Selection. ship a SMALL explicit table (String, Int,
    Boolean, Path, List[String], List[Path]) and make everything else a
    hard registration error naming the type it could not build. do NOT
    add a reflective bean-style fallback: a wrong coercion that
    type-checks is worse than a refusal. generic parameter types survive
    erasure in the Signature attribute, so getGenericParameterTypes
    distinguishes List[Path] from List[String] — VERIFIED 2026-08-19 by
    javap on our own mcp.jar (`find_section(List<Doc_Catalog$Heading>,
    String)`), so the table can key on the real type and not on `List`.

D3  result rendering: call toString on the returned value, EXCEPT where
    the target type has a known renderer. rationale: a Report or a
    case class renders as scala noise, which is useless to an agent.
    where the plan cannot do better, say so in the tool description
    rather than pretending the output is structured. (this is the
    weakest part of the design and the honest place to admit it — a
    tool whose output is `Linter$Report@3f2a` has advertised a
    capability it does not have.)

D4  overloads are REJECTED, not resolved. getMethod needs parameter
    types; the declaration supplies names and arity. if more than one
    method matches, fail at registration listing the candidates and
    require the declaration to disambiguate. guessing here would bind a
    tool to a method the author never meant.

A1  [scala unit] a target naming a missing class, a missing method, or
    a class with no MODULE$ (i.e. a scala CLASS, not an object) each
    fail at REGISTRATION with a message naming which of the three went
    wrong. the three messages must differ — "reflection failed" is not
    actionable.

A2  [scala unit] the class/method split is ambiguous and the resolution
    is longest-class-first: `a.b.C.d` tries class `a.b.C` method `d`
    before class `a.b` method `C.d`. a target that resolves to neither
    lists both attempts. (this is a small thing that will otherwise
    produce baffling errors for nested objects.)

A3  [scala unit] MODULE$ is how a scala object is reached — VERIFIED
    2026-08-19 against mcp.jar: `object Doc_Catalog` compiles to class
    `isabelle.mcp.Doc_Catalog$` carrying
    `public static final isabelle.mcp.Doc_Catalog$ MODULE$`. the test
    pins this against our OWN jar so it fails loudly if a scala version
    bump changes the encoding.

A4  [bridge] the AMBIENT fill works: a target taking a
    Document.Snapshot is called with a `theory` argument only, and the
    snapshot it receives is the one the session holds for that theory —
    asserted by having the target return something only a real snapshot
    can produce (its command count), cross-checked against the same
    count read through an existing tool. this is D1's load-bearing
    claim and the one that makes the linter reachable.

A5  [bridge] the session a Scala.Fun receives IS the server's live
    session, not a fresh or stale one. asserted by resolving a theory
    that was loaded mid-session, after the Fun was registered.

T1  [bridge] the minimal round trip: scala_mcp_tool over a
    String-in/String-out method of our OWN jar, declared in a theory,
    advertised in tools/list, called, correct value back.

T2  [scala unit] the coercion table (D2): each supported type converts,
    and an unsupported type fails at registration naming the type.

T3  [scala unit] overload rejection (D4): a target with two matching
    methods fails at registration listing both.

T4  [bridge] error mapping end to end: a target that throws surfaces as
    an mcp isError with the CAUSE unwrapped, not a bare
    InvocationTargetException. the server survives and answers the next
    call.

Q1  OPEN: whether the linter integration ships as a test at all. it is
    not installed here (LINTER_FINDINGS.md open questions), a release
    matching Isabelle2025-2 exists (Isabelle2025-2-v1.0.0, 2026-05-04),
    and registering it is `isabelle components -u` with a prebuilt jar —
    no scala_build, which matters given the read-only-jar problem under
    the flatpak. settle by installing it once and seeing whether the
    round trip works; if it does, the case ships SKIPPED-IF-ABSENT so
    the suite stays green on a machine without the component.

Q2  OPEN: whether MCP.dynamic_call should cache resolved Method handles.
    reflection lookup per call is wasteful but the target set is tiny
    and correctness comes first; measure before caching, and note that
    a cache keyed on the target string must be invalidated if a
    post-boot jar load is ever added (LINTER_FINDINGS.md section 6.3 —
    post-boot loading is visible only to its own loader, so a cache
    would silently serve the wrong class).

Q4  OPEN, and it decides how much of D1 actually works: do parameter
    NAMES survive into the bytecode? D1 says parameters are filled "by
    parameter NAME", but javac only emits names with -parameters and
    scala 3's default here is unverified — nor is it known what
    Isabelle's own scala_build passes. TYPES are confirmed present
    (A3's javap evidence), names are not. if names are absent the fill
    degrades to POSITIONAL, i.e. tier 1's contract with types as a
    partial check on top — still worth building, but a weaker claim
    than this plan currently makes, and the plan's prose must be
    corrected rather than quietly reinterpreted. one javap -v run
    against mcp.jar settles it; do that BEFORE step 3.

Q3  OPEN, inherited from plans/scala_mcp_fun A3: the blocking cost is
    WORSE here than for tier 1, because a realistic target (linting a
    whole theory) runs for seconds rather than microseconds with the
    prover thread held. this may make async MCP.run_tool
    (plans/ml_builtin_migration) a PREREQUISITE rather than a
    companion. decide after A3's measurement in the tier-1 plan.

done when
---------

- A1..A5, T1..T4 green; a scala_mcp_tool row over our own jar works end
  to end; Q1 settled either way and recorded.
- grep gate: no occurrence of "linter" in mcp/src or mcp/Tools. the
  point of this plan is that the linter needs no code of ours, so a
  mention in source is the failure signal.
